From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: sun8i: add A733 (sun60iw2) support

The A733's thermal block is register-compatible with the H6 layout: every
SUN50I_H6_* offset in this driver lands on a live, correctly shaped register on
this part, and the vendor driver programs CTRL0 and MFC with the same constants
this driver writes (0x01DF002F and 0x00000005, read back on a running board).
THS_PER (this driver's SUN50I_H6_THS_PC) is the exception: the reused
sun50i_h6_thermal_init() writes 365, the H6 value, where the vendor's
sun60iw2_thermal_init() writes 28 and the UM's init sequence 0x3A = 58
(UM V1.00 7.1.6.1 Step 5, p. 344). At 365 each sensor updates every
1.25 s instead of 0.10 s (vendor) or 0.20 s (UM); `thermal-sun8i-a733-filter-the-trend-fed-to-step-wise` measures and works
with that cadence, it does not change it.

Three things differ and are handled here.

Five sensors, not four, so MAX_SENSOR_NUM grows by one. The ENABLE register
reads 0x0000001F on hardware, and DATA, CDATA and ALARM each have exactly five
populated entries -- 0xD4 upwards reads zero.

Which block each sensor sits in: the UM says 0 = CPUB, 1 = DDR, 2 = NPU,
3 = CPUL, 4 = GPU. ⚠ The two CPU entries are measured the other way
round -- ths 0 is at the A55 and ths 3 at the A76 -- by a per-cluster stimulus
replicated on two dates (one of them the 2026-08-15 series on the 8 GiB
board). Nothing in this driver depends on it: the sensor index is passed
straight through from the DT cell to temp_data_base + 4 * id, so the mapping is
a device-tree question and `arm64-dts-a733-thermal-zones-and-cpu-throttling` is where it is decided and argued. It is
recorded here only because this comment used to assert the UM order as fact.

A second clock. The ADC runs off a 24 MHz conversion clock whose CCU reset
default is OFF, so has_mod_clk is set alongside has_bus_clk_reset.

The transfer function is the one value in this patch that is not from a
document: the UM does not print the raw-to-Celsius formula. It was first
regressed on silicon -- two straight segments, exact on 34 of 35 sweep points,
all five sensors on two dies, between 39 and 74 C -- and it is the vendor's
formula, sun60iw2_calc_temp() in the vendor sunxi_thermal.c: same slopes
(-62 and -59 mC/LSB), same intercepts (174964 = 2822 * 62 and 167265 =
2835 * 59), same breakpoint (raw > 1769), checked in the code and against the
vendor driver's register/report pairs on three kernels (`arm64-dts-a733-thermal-zones-and-cpu-throttling`). So our scale is
the vendor's scale over the whole range, not only the fitted one. What is open
is the absolute accuracy of Allwinner's formula above 74 C, and that can only
be checked with an external thermometer; nothing on this board can. The fuses
do not settle it: they carry one calibration point, ft_temp = 25.0 C, which
fixes a per-sensor offset, not the slope.

No calibration cell is wired. The A733 does carry per-die factory data in the
SID fuses, and the fuse-to-CDATA transform is known, though not from the UM
(which only says to write the eFuse value to THSn_CDATA): the vendor's
sun60iw2_ths_calibrate() reads SID 0x44/0x48/0x4c as a packed 12-bit array
(ft_temp, then the five sensors) and writes cdata = 0x800 - delta, and that
arithmetic reproduced the live CDATA registers of a vendor board 5 of 5 (2026-08-16). This driver does not
implement it, and sun8i_ths_calibrate() is written to continue without it.
The measured cost of shipping on the 0x800 reset default is -12..+7 LSB
across five sensors on two dies = -0.74..+0.43 C, an order of magnitude
inside the sensor's own +-3 C.

needs_sram stays false: that is an H616 erratum, not this part.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/sun8i_thermal.c
@@ -25,7 +25,7 @@
 
 #include "thermal_hwmon.h"
 
-#define MAX_SENSOR_NUM	4
+#define MAX_SENSOR_NUM	5
 
 #define FT_TEMP_MASK				GENMASK(11, 0)
 #define TEMP_CALIB_MASK				GENMASK(11, 0)
@@ -114,6 +114,38 @@
 		return -1590 * reg / 10 + 276000;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A733. The UM extract this port was written from does not print the raw-to-
+ * temperature formula, so it was derived on silicon instead: a vendor-BSP board
+ * was swept from idle to load and its reported millidegrees regressed against
+ * the raw DATA words read back in the same instant. 34 of 35 points fall
+ * exactly on one of two straight lines, and the cold line reproduces all five
+ * sensors on two different dies to the millidegree:
+ *
+ *   raw 1778..2191  (39.1..64.7 C)   temp_mC = 174964 - 62 * raw
+ *   raw 1586..1761  (63.4..73.7 C)   temp_mC = 167265 - 59 * raw
+ *
+ * The breakpoint sits near raw 1770. A two-segment response is not unusual
+ * here -- sun50i_h5_calc_temp() above switches on the raw word for the same
+ * reason.
+ *
+ * This is the vendor's formula, not a fit of ours: sun60iw2_calc_temp() in the
+ * vendor sunxi_thermal.c has the same slopes (-62, -59), the same intercepts
+ * (2822 * 62 = 174964, 2835 * 59 = 167265) and the same breakpoint (raw >
+ * 1769), so this scale is the vendor's above 73.7 C too. What stays open is
+ * the absolute accuracy of that formula above 74 C, and only an external
+ * thermometer can check it. The SID fuses carry one calibration point
+ * (ft_temp = 25.0 C): an offset per sensor, not the slope.
+ */
+static int sun60i_a733_calc_temp(struct ths_device *tmdev,
+				 int id, int reg)
+{
+	if (reg >= 1770)
+		return 174964 - 62 * reg;
+
+	return 167265 - 59 * reg;
+}
+
 static int sun8i_ths_get_temp(struct thermal_zone_device *tz, int *temp)
 {
 	struct tsensor *s = thermal_zone_device_priv(tz);
@@ -709,6 +741,46 @@
 	.calc_temp = sun8i_ths_calc_temp,
 };
 
+/*
+ * A733 (sun60iw2). Five sensors. The UM places them
+ * 0 = CPUB, 1 = DDR,
+ * 2 = NPU, 3 = CPUL, 4 = GPU; the two CPU entries are measured the other way
+ * round (ths 0 at the A55, ths 3 at the A76) and the device tree follows the
+ * measurement -- see the thermal-zones comment in
+ * sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts. This driver is index-agnostic either way:
+ * the DT cell becomes sensor[i].id and addresses temp_data_base + 4 * i.
+ * Register-compatible with the H6 layout: every SUN50I_H6_*
+ * offset lands on a live, correctly shaped register on this part, and the
+ * vendor driver programs CTRL0 and MFC with the same constants this driver
+ * writes. THS_PER (SUN50I_H6_THS_PC) is not the same: the reused
+ * sun50i_h6_thermal_init() writes 365, the vendor 28, the UM's init sequence
+ * 0x3A = 58 -- 1.25 s per sensor update here against 0.10 s and 0.20 s.
+ *
+ * Two clocks: the bus gate, and a 24 MHz conversion clock that the ADC needs
+ * and whose CCU reset default is OFF -- hence has_mod_clk as well as
+ * has_bus_clk_reset.
+ *
+ * No calibration cell. The A733 does have per-die factory data in the SID
+ * fuses, and the fuse-to-CDATA transform is known from the vendor's
+ * sun60iw2_ths_calibrate() (SID 0x44/0x48/0x4c, cdata = 0x800 - delta),
+ * verified 5 of 5 against a vendor board's live CDATA -- but this driver does
+ * not implement it, and sun8i_ths_calibrate() continues without it. Measured
+ * cost of shipping uncalibrated on this part: CDATA reads 0x7F4..0x807 across
+ * five sensors on two dies, i.e. -12..+7 LSB from the 0x800 reset default, or
+ * -0.74..+0.43 C at 62 mC/LSB -- an order of magnitude inside the sensor's own
+ * +-3 C rating. Calibration is a refinement here, not a dependency.
+ */
+static const struct ths_thermal_chip sun60i_a733_ths = {
+	.sensor_num = 5,
+	.has_bus_clk_reset = true,
+	.has_mod_clk = true,
+	.temp_data_base = SUN50I_H6_THS_TEMP_DATA,
+	.calibrate = sun50i_h6_ths_calibrate,
+	.init = sun50i_h6_thermal_init,
+	.irq_ack = sun50i_h6_irq_ack,
+	.calc_temp = sun60i_a733_calc_temp,
+};
+
 static const struct of_device_id of_ths_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun8i-a83t-ths", .data = &sun8i_a83t_ths },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun8i-h3-ths", .data = &sun8i_h3_ths },
@@ -719,6 +791,7 @@
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h6-ths", .data = &sun50i_h6_ths },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun20i-d1-ths", .data = &sun20i_d1_ths },
 	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h616-ths", .data = &sun50i_h616_ths },
+	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-ths", .data = &sun60i_a733_ths },
 	{ /* sentinel */ },
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, of_ths_match);
